<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignaz Semmelweis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignaz Semmelweis was a Hungarian physician who, working at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-general-hospital">Vienna General Hospital</a> in 1847, showed that childbed fever was greatly reduced when doctors washed their hands in chlorinated lime before examining women in labour. His findings were widely rejected in his lifetime, but they were vindicated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease">germ theory</a> of disease, and he is honoured as a pioneer of antiseptic practice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignaz-semmelweis.jpg" alt="Ignaz Semmelweis" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: József Borsos / Albert Doctor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borsos_%26_Doctor_Semmelweis_Ign%C3%A1c_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ignaz Semmelweis was born on 1 July 1818 in the Tabán neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, the fifth of ten children of a prosperous grocer family. His father József Semmelweis ran a wholesale business for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and consumer goods, and his mother was Teréz Müller. Ignaz began studying law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> in the autumn of 1837 but switched to medicine and received his doctorate in 1844. Having failed to win a post in internal medicine, he specialised in obstetrics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 1 July 1846 Semmelweis became assistant to Professor Johann Klein in the First Obstetrical Clinic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-general-hospital">Vienna General Hospital</a>. Its maternal mortality from puerperal fever was about 10 per cent, against under 4 per cent in the Second Clinic, and women begged not to be admitted to the First. His breakthrough came in 1847 after the death of his friend Jakob Kolletschka, who was pricked by a student's scalpel during an autopsy and showed a pathology like that of the dying women. Semmelweis concluded that he and his students carried cadaverous particles on their hands from the autopsy room to patients, and he introduced hand washing in chlorinated lime solutions.</p><p>Klein did not extend his term, and Semmelweis had to leave the clinic on 20 March 1849. On 20 May 1851 he took an unpaid post heading the obstetric ward of the Szent Rókus Hospital in Pest, where from 1851 to 1855 only eight of 933 mothers died of childbed fever. His book Etiology, Concept and Prophylaxis of Childbed Fever appeared in 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His claim that the disease had a single cause, a lack of cleanliness, ran against the doctrine of the four humours, and he could not give a theoretical explanation. Some doctors were offended by the suggestion that their hands were unclean. After unfavourable reviews of his book he wrote open letters that were polemical and offensive. In 1865 he was committed to the provincial asylum at Döbling and died there on 13 August 1865, 14 days later, of septic shock, possibly after a beating by guards.</p><p>His practice won wide acceptance only years later, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur">Louis Pasteur</a>'s germ theory gave it an explanation and Joseph Lister developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiseptic-surgery">antiseptic surgery</a>. He is called the saviour of mothers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ignaz Semmelweis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In science, <b>Ignaz Semmelweis</b> showed at the Vienna General Hospital that handwashing prevented childbed fever, and Gregor Mendel presented his experiments on plant hybridisation in Brünn in 1865, laying the foundations of genetics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tabán, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Oberdöbling, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.49,19.04&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Tabán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ignaz Semmelweis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4370 6009h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4370 6009h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.49,19.04&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
